      "reasoning_content": "Key findings:\n\n1. `ITVIE._VALID_URL = 'https?://(?:www\\\\.)?itv\\\\.com/hub/[^/]+/(?P<id>[0-9a-zA-Z]+)'` - so URL like `https://www.itv.com/hub/liar/2a4547a0012` gives id `2a4547a0012`.\n\n2. `_json_ld`: For TVEpisode, requires `'@context' in e` (any value!). Then:\n   - `episode` = name\n   - `episode_number` = int_or_none(episodeNumber)\n   - `description` = unescapeHTML(description)\n   - `title` = episode_name if not info.get('title') and episode_name\n   - partOfSeason -> season, season_number\n   - partOfSeries -> series\n   \n   Returns dict filtered for non-None values. Note: `if '@context' in e` - just presence check, any value works. The itv code sets `item['@context'] = 'http://schema.org'`.\n   \n   Important: `_json_ld` iterates `for e in json_ld:` where json_ld is a list or dict->wrapped in list. Since item is a dict, wrapped in [item]. Then `if '@context' in e:` - yes. item_type = 'TVEpisode' -> updates info. Then `video = e.get('video')` - None. Then `if expected_type is None: continue` -> continues loop, ends. Returns filtered info.\n\n   So for TVEpisode with name 'JSON-LD Episode', description, episodeNumber 6, partOfSeries {'@type': 'TVSeries', 'name': 'Liar'}, partOfSeason {'@type': 'TVSeason', 'name': 'Series 2', 'seasonNumber': 2}:\n   info = {'episode': 'JSON-LD Episode', 'episode_number': 6, 'description': '...', 'title': 'JSON-LD Episode', 'season': 'Series 2', 'season_number': 2, 'series': 'Liar'}.\n\n3. `_search_json_ld`: I'll mock it to return {} for the fallback test. Note its real behavior: finds all JSON-LD scripts, parses, calls _json_ld with the LIST. Interesting - if I didn't mock it, the BreadcrumbList would be passed as a list to _json_ld; BreadcrumbList type isn't handled -> info stays {} -> returns default {}. So actually NOT mocking would also work: `_search_json_ld(webpage, video_id, default={})` with a BreadcrumbList JSON-LD returns {} (since _json_ld returns {} for BreadcrumbList), then fallba